{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 399,
    "dDurationMs": 3831,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Perhaps you’ve seen videos of how the planets\nof the solar system move through the universe"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4230,
    "dDurationMs": 6840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in this cool helix. But not only are these\nmisleading, Earth’s real motion - your real"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 11070,
    "dDurationMs": 8930,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "motion through the universe - is way more\ncomplicated and way more interesting."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 20000,
    "dDurationMs": 7990,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This video of the planets moving in a corkscrew\npattern isn’t exactly wrong, but it’s"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 27990,
    "dDurationMs": 5390,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "often presented in the most misleading sense\n- by suggesting that the “old” idea of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 33380,
    "dDurationMs": 6109,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "planets orbiting the Sun is wrong - and that\nwe really move in a cool vortex. This is misleading"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 39489,
    "dDurationMs": 5921,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "because it suggests that one frame of reference\n- the one where we see the helix - is fundamentally"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 45410,
    "dDurationMs": 5579,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "better than any others. Today I’m going\nto say a word or two against this reference"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 50989,
    "dDurationMs": 5271,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "frame chauvinism, and then talk about how\nthe Earth REALLY moves through the universe,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 56260,
    "dDurationMs": 3910,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which involves a surprising amount of awesome\nastrophysics.."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 60170,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To start, I want you to imagine you’re in\na ship moving steadily on a perfectly smooth"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 66330,
    "dDurationMs": 5320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ocean in an enclosed cabin. The portholes\nare closed and there’s no way you can tell"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 71650,
    "dDurationMs": 5350,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "how fast you’re moving. Or even that you’re\nmoving at all. This is the thought experiment"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 77000,
    "dDurationMs": 6170,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that Galileo Galilei described in his 1632\nbook Dialogue Concerning the Two Chief World"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 83170,
    "dDurationMs": 7540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Systems to express what we now call Galilean\nrelativity. It’s the idea that there’s"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 90710,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "no single best frame of reference from which\nto define the concept of stillness. There’s"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 95750,
    "dDurationMs": 6340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "no absolute rest-frame. All non-accelerating\nframes - also called inertial frames - can"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 102090,
    "dDurationMs": 5029,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "be taken as motionless as far as the laws\nof physics are concerned."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 107119,
    "dDurationMs": 5651,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So the view of the Solar System tracing this\nhelix through space IS a valid frame of reference,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 112770,
    "dDurationMs": 5610,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but you know what else is a valid frame of\nreference? This one. Where the planets orbit"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 118380,
    "dDurationMs": 5620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the Sun just like you were always taught.\nAt least on timescales of less than millions"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 124000,
    "dDurationMs": 6200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of years. OK, meme debunking achieved. Let’s\nmove on to the interesting astrophysics and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 130200,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "talk about how Earth is really moving, and\nwhat we can learn from that."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 135080,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Given this Galilean relativity of motion,\nis it even meaningful to say how the Earth"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 140920,
    "dDurationMs": 6670,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is moving? Well, there may be no such thing\nas a single fundamental definition of motionlessness,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 147590,
    "dDurationMs": 7140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but that doesn’t mean all reference frames\nare equal. We choose a reference frame that’s"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 154730,
    "dDurationMs": 6780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "most useful for the question at hand. For\nexample, if your brain is subconsciously computing"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 161510,
    "dDurationMs": 3970,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the trajectory of a ball flying through the\nair in order to catch it, you’re not going"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 165480,
    "dDurationMs": 5240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to take into account the fact that the surface\nof the Earth is moving at 1000 km/hr or so"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 170720,
    "dDurationMs": 5460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "due to its rotation. You can treat your patch\nof the Earth’s surface as inertial for that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 176180,
    "dDurationMs": 1059,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "calculation."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 177239,
    "dDurationMs": 4110,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If NASA wants to send a robot to Mars, it\nprobably doesn’t want to use the frame of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 181349,
    "dDurationMs": 5621,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "reference of Cape Canaveral, or even of the\nEarth’s center to do its celestial mechanics."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 186970,
    "dDurationMs": 5970,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It uses an inertial frame of reference of\nthe solar system. But NASA gets to ignore"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 192940,
    "dDurationMs": 3469,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the fact that the Sun is racing around the\ngalaxy at 230 km/s."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 196409,
    "dDurationMs": 5481,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So if we want to answer the question “how\nis the Earth really moving” we need to choose"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 201890,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the useful frames of reference. Let’s start\nwith the frame of reference of our familiar"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 207530,
    "dDurationMs": 6420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "depictions of the motions of the planets - one\nroughly fixed on the Sun. One thing is inaccurate"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 213950,
    "dDurationMs": 4450,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "about this depiction - and that’s the idea\nthat the planets are orbiting the Sun, while"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 218400,
    "dDurationMs": 5750,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the Sun is fixed relative to the planets.\nThe planets tug on the Sun just as it tugs"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 224150,
    "dDurationMs": 3490,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on the planets, causing the Sun to move."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 227640,
    "dDurationMs": 6260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The best reference frame to describe motion\nwithin the solar system is that of the barycenter"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 233900,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- the center of mass. This is the real point\nthat everything in the solar system is orbiting"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 240940,
    "dDurationMs": 6909,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": ". From the reference frame of the barycenter,\nthe Sun executes this complex pirouette, mostly"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 247849,
    "dDurationMs": 5761,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in response to the outweighed gravitational\npulls of Jupiter and Saturn. The Solar System’s"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 253610,
    "dDurationMs": 4879,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "barycenter is sometimes beneath the Sun’s\nsurface, but when the two giant planets are"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 258489,
    "dDurationMs": 3851,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "even vaguely lined up it can be quite a way\noutside of the Sun."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 262340,
    "dDurationMs": 5380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So how does this affect Earth’s orbit? The\nbarycenter’s motion is slower than Earth’s"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 267720,
    "dDurationMs": 5539,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "orbit. So this causes Earth’s elliptical\norbit to stretch and squish very slightly"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 273259,
    "dDurationMs": 6211,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on the timescale of Jupiter and Saturn’s\nyears - 5 and 12 Earth years respectively."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 279470,
    "dDurationMs": 5590,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And the pull of the other planets also causes\nthe orientation of that ellipse to rotate"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 285060,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "around the Sun over 1000’s of years. We\ntalked about that effect in detail, and its"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 290220,
    "dDurationMs": 5000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "connection to ice ages, when we discussed\nthe Milankovic cycles."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 295220,
    "dDurationMs": 6130,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So that’s Earth’s motion within the solar\nsystem - complicated, but not as complicated"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 301350,
    "dDurationMs": 6520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as its motion through the galaxy. That galactic\nmotion is also much harder to figure out."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 307870,
    "dDurationMs": 4380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In the solar system, there are relatively\nfew bodies whose gravity you need to account"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 312250,
    "dDurationMs": 5500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for, and anyway, the gravity is massively\ndominated by the Sun. The source of the Milky"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 317750,
    "dDurationMs": 5910,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Way’s gravitational field isn’t dominated\nby one object - everything orbits in the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 323660,
    "dDurationMs": 5750,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "summed gravitational fields of everything\nelse. That makes things tricky, but it also"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 329410,
    "dDurationMs": 5030,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "means we can learn an enormous amount about\nour Galaxy’s complex structure just by tracking"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 334440,
    "dDurationMs": 2220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the motion of its constituents."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 336660,
    "dDurationMs": 5030,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The Solar system is moving at about 230km/s\nrelative to the center of the Milky Way - give"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 341690,
    "dDurationMs": 5730,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or take. That means a single orbit takes almost\n230 million years. The last time the earth"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 347420,
    "dDurationMs": 6029,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "was on this side of the galaxy, dinosaurs\nwandered Pangea and trilobites had just gone"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 353449,
    "dDurationMs": 1551,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "extinct."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 355000,
    "dDurationMs": 5810,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To describe the Sun’s motion through the\ngalaxy we need to choose a new reference frame."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 360810,
    "dDurationMs": 5389,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We could choose the center of the galaxy - but\nthat’s actually very difficult to pinpoint."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 366199,
    "dDurationMs": 6021,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Instead we tend to describe the Sun’s complicated\nmotion relative to a much simpler hypothetical"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 372220,
    "dDurationMs": 8069,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "orbit - what we call the Local Standard of\nRest. The LSR is the reference frame that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 380289,
    "dDurationMs": 4970,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the Sun would have if it were executing a\nperfectly circular orbit from its current"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 385259,
    "dDurationMs": 6411,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "position. This allows us to describe how “not\ncircular” the Sun’s orbit is. There’s"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 391670,
    "dDurationMs": 7440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "also a good physical motivation. Almost every\nstar in the Milky Way disk starts in an orbit"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 399110,
    "dDurationMs": 5900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that’s nearly circular. That’s because\nthe clouds of gas from which stars form get"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 405010,
    "dDurationMs": 8279,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "nudged into circular orbits due to friction\nwith other gas - friction that stars don’t"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 413289,
    "dDurationMs": 6030,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "experience. After a star forms in the same\ncircular orbit as its birth cloud, it gradually"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 419319,
    "dDurationMs": 5871,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "gets nudged off that orbit by a miriad of\ngravitational kicks from other objects in"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 425190,
    "dDurationMs": 1530,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the Milky Way."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 426720,
    "dDurationMs": 5690,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Determining the speed of the sun with respect\nto the LSR is just a matter of finding lots"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 432410,
    "dDurationMs": 6969,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of new, young stars that formed recently and\nare still in their birth orbits, and taking"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 439379,
    "dDurationMs": 5630,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "an average of how they’re moving relative\nto us. From these measurements we find that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 445009,
    "dDurationMs": 6361,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the sun is going a little bit faster than\nit should be for a perfectly circular orbit-"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 451370,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "relative to the local standard of rest, the\nSun is drifting “forwards” at about 5"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 457690,
    "dDurationMs": 7379,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "km/s. It’s also drifting ‘inwards’ toward\nthe galactic center at about 8 km/s and moving"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 465069,
    "dDurationMs": 7211,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "up and out of the disk at about 7 km/s. This\ntiny ‘peculiar motion’ as it’s called,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 472280,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "has enormous consequences for the path that\nthe sun and solar system take through the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 477160,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "galaxy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 478160,
    "dDurationMs": 4599,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For starters, this slow drift toward the galactic\ncenter doesn’t mean we’re going to fall"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 482759,
    "dDurationMs": 7940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "into Sagittarius A*. The Sun is trying to\nexecute a slightly elliptical orbit around"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 490699,
    "dDurationMs": 5911,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the galaxy, and currently it’s moving closer\nto the galactic center. But the Milky Way’s"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 496610,
    "dDurationMs": 5350,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mass is spread out through the entire galaxy\nrather than concentrated in the center like"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 501960,
    "dDurationMs": 5650,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in the solar system. That means simple elliptical\norbits aren’t possible. Instead, the Sun"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 507610,
    "dDurationMs": 7910,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "traces out this pretty flower pattern over\nmany orbits. Geometrically it’s ‘epicyclic’"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 515520,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- the shape that a smaller wheel makes when\nrolling on a bigger wheel."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 519960,
    "dDurationMs": 5080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The slight upward motion of the Sun is perhaps\neven cooler - and certainly quite a bit more"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 525040,
    "dDurationMs": 5700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "impactful. I already mentioned that we’re\nmoving up out of the galactic disk at around"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 530740,
    "dDurationMs": 7270,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "7 km/s. We are currently a few dozen lightyears\nabove the middle, although it’s hard to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 538010,
    "dDurationMs": 4269,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "know exactly where the center of the galactic\ndisk is. We’re not in danger of escaping"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 542279,
    "dDurationMs": 7091,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the galaxy. With more matter below than above\nus, the disk’s gravity is slowing our upwards"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 549370,
    "dDurationMs": 6529,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "motion. In a few million years we’ll have\nclawed our way to around 300 light years above"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 555899,
    "dDurationMs": 5961,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the disk center before our upwards motion\nslows to a halt, and we begin to fall back"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 561860,
    "dDurationMs": 5580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in. We’ll plummet through the disk, overshoot,\nand pop out the other side. We execute one"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 567440,
    "dDurationMs": 6670,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of these graceful leaps roughly once every\n60 million years, so a few times per galactic"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 574110,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "orbit."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 575110,
    "dDurationMs": 3849,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This vertical oscillation is more than just\na curiosity. Some astronomers think it could"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 578959,
    "dDurationMs": 5421,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "be directly tied to mass extinctions on the\nearth. You know the dinosaurs went extinct"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 584380,
    "dDurationMs": 1720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "60 million years ago."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 586100,
    "dDurationMs": 6060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The center of the disk is a more dangerous\nplace for the solar system due to the higher"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 592160,
    "dDurationMs": 6660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "density of stars. It puts us at a bigger risk\nof a nearby supernova, or close encounters"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 598820,
    "dDurationMs": 5550,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with other massive objects that could destabilize\ndebris in the outer solar system that could"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 604370,
    "dDurationMs": 3870,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "impact the earth. But that’s a story for\nanother time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 608240,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This bouncing motion of the sun and other\nstars is also a useful scientific tool - it"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 613880,
    "dDurationMs": 6090,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "gives us a way to test different theories\nof the mysterious dark matter which is invisible"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 619970,
    "dDurationMs": 5550,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and has only been detected by its strong gravitational\ninfluence. There are many different models"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 625520,
    "dDurationMs": 5780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for what type of particle or object dark matter\nis made up of, and these models make different"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 631300,
    "dDurationMs": 4990,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "predictions for how dark matter interacts\nwith itself. In the more mainstream models"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 636290,
    "dDurationMs": 5770,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it interacts with itself almost not at all,\nwhich means it stays very puffy and spread"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 642060,
    "dDurationMs": 6890,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "out. But some dark matter candidates might\nexperience very weak self-interactions, resulting"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 648950,
    "dDurationMs": 5210,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in a force similar to friction and enabling\ndark matter to accumulate in the disk of the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 654160,
    "dDurationMs": 5290,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "galaxy, that would lead to a more massive\ndisk than the case where dark matter is more"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 659450,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "spread out. So by weighing the disk we can\npotentially test these types of dark matter"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 666250,
    "dDurationMs": 6010,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "models. And we can do that by looking at the\nvertical oscillations of stars. That’s because"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 672260,
    "dDurationMs": 6530,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the mass of the disk determines how far stars\ncan rise in these vertical oscillations. Measuring"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 678790,
    "dDurationMs": 7520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "vertical speeds and maximum heights for nearby\nstars has so far told us that there’s no"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 686310,
    "dDurationMs": 5790,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "evidence of any extra density of dark matter\nin the disk, which supports non-interacting"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 692100,
    "dDurationMs": 1179,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "dark matter models."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 693279,
    "dDurationMs": 4401,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Okay, now we know how the sun moves through\nthe galaxy, but what about the solar system"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 697680,
    "dDurationMs": 5790,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as a whole? The plane of the planet’s orbits\n- also called the ecliptic plane - is tilted"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 703470,
    "dDurationMs": 5330,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "by about 60 degrees. That's why we see this\nsort of squished corkscrew pattern as the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 708800,
    "dDurationMs": 2620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "planets move through the galaxy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 711420,
    "dDurationMs": 5080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The orbits of the planets cause them to spend\nhalf their year ahead of the sun in the galactic"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 716500,
    "dDurationMs": 4990,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "orbit, while they lag behind it during the\nother half. In the case of the earth, it’s"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 721490,
    "dDurationMs": 5950,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the furthest ahead of the sun in September,\nwhile in March it’s farthest behind. Also,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 727440,
    "dDurationMs": 5510,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for half the year Earth is moving in the same\ndirection as the Sun through the galaxy so"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 732950,
    "dDurationMs": 5210,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "their velocities add together, with the maximum\nspeed in June. While in the other half of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 738160,
    "dDurationMs": 4780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the year we’re moving backwards relative\nto the Sun’s motion, so in December Earth’s"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 742940,
    "dDurationMs": 2380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "galactic motion is the slowest."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 745320,
    "dDurationMs": 5730,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This change in Earth’s galactic speed also\nhappens to be useful for testing - guess what"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 751050,
    "dDurationMs": 7030,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- dark matter models. If dark matter is some\nexotic particle, then it must be passing through"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 758080,
    "dDurationMs": 5420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the Earth as we move through the galaxy. And\nthe faster we move, the more dark matter we"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 763500,
    "dDurationMs": 3949,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "should sweep through, in the same way that\nmore rain hits your windshield the faster"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 767449,
    "dDurationMs": 6651,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you drive. Dark matter experiments should\nsee more events in June compared to December."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 774100,
    "dDurationMs": 6650,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In fact, physicists at the DAMA experiment\nin Gran Sasso in Italy actually may have detected"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 780750,
    "dDurationMs": 4639,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "this signal though that’s still disputed-\npartly because other detectors haven’t been"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 785389,
    "dDurationMs": 1890,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "able to reproduce it yet."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 787279,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Okay, just one more bone to pick about this\nso-called vortex motion. Currently the ecliptic"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 793279,
    "dDurationMs": 4791,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is almost face-on compared to our Sun’s\norbital motion, so the corkscrew is valid."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 798070,
    "dDurationMs": 5530,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But due to conservation of angular momentum,\nthe plane of the solar system won’t ‘turn’"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 803600,
    "dDurationMs": 5270,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with the Sun’s orbit. After a quarter of\na galactic orbit, the ecliptic plane will"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 808870,
    "dDurationMs": 4839,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "be edge-on - the solar system will be like\na great wheel rolling in the direction of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 813709,
    "dDurationMs": 1081,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the sun’s orbit."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 814790,
    "dDurationMs": 4770,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We now have a picture of how the Solar system\nreally moves through the galaxy. But how do"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 819560,
    "dDurationMs": 6579,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we move through the universe as a whole? The\nMilky Way is pulled by the gravitational fields"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 826139,
    "dDurationMs": 4481,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the masses around it. We’re racing towards\nthe Andromeda galaxy at a couple of hundred"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 830620,
    "dDurationMs": 5080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "kilometers per second, while our entire local\ngroup - Andromeda, the Milky Way, and the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 835700,
    "dDurationMs": 6540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "other little galaxies around us - are being\npulled at several hundred km/s towards a mysterious"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 842240,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "overdensity in the clustering of galaxies\ncalled the Great Attractor. At this point,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 848480,
    "dDurationMs": 4410,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it becomes really tricky to define a single\nframe of reference to precisely define our"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 852890,
    "dDurationMs": 2540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "relative velocity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 855430,
    "dDurationMs": 6250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Except we do have one last recourse - a reference\nframe granted to us by the earliest epochs"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 861680,
    "dDurationMs": 6290,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the universe. The reference frame of the\ncosmic microwave background. This is the faint,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 867970,
    "dDurationMs": 5340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "radio-wavelength light that was emitted by\nthe hot hydrogen that filled the universe"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 873310,
    "dDurationMs": 5900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "before the first stars were born. Those hydrogen\natoms were moving in all different directions,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 879210,
    "dDurationMs": 6340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but they had an average velocity. If you were\nthere near the beginning, and you were moving"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 885550,
    "dDurationMs": 5450,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "at the average velocity of those atoms, then\nthe light produced by those atoms would look"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 891000,
    "dDurationMs": 6110,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the same in all directions. However if you\nhad any different velocity, then the light"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 897110,
    "dDurationMs": 6159,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "hitting you from ahead would be a bit more\nenergetic - a bit bluer. Light hitting you"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 903269,
    "dDurationMs": 3641,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "from behind it would be less energetic, or\nmore red."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 906910,
    "dDurationMs": 7349,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well, those atoms are mostly in stars now,\nbut the light they produced is still everywhere."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 914259,
    "dDurationMs": 6461,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The cosmic microwave background defines a\nrest frame for the universe. And we can check"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 920720,
    "dDurationMs": 10679,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "our reference frame compared to it. It turns\nout that we are hurtling at 368 (+ or - 2) km/s"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 931399,
    "dDurationMs": 5951,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "relative to the cosmic rest frame - we’ve\nbeen tugged from the cosmic average velocity"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 937350,
    "dDurationMs": 5090,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "by vast overdensities of galaxies hundreds\nof millions of light years away."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 942440,
    "dDurationMs": 6769,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So that is how you are currently moving through\nthe universe. It’s wildly dizzying in all"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 949209,
    "dDurationMs": 6130,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but the reference frame of your own body.\nAt the most extreme, you are wheeling in squished corkscrew"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 955339,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that shifts to a rolling wheel as it dips\nabove and below the galactic disk tracing"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 960699,
    "dDurationMs": 6741,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "flower petals around the Milky Way, which\nin turn forms a galaxy-scale helix, the sum"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 967440,
    "dDurationMs": 5730,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "total of our wheeling dance across space time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 973170,
    "dDurationMs": 4300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In our last episode we explored the possibility that life could be based on silicon"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 977470,
    "dDurationMs": 7210,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "rather than carbon. I concluded that\ncarbon is way better than silicon for life, but there was some deep"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 984680,
    "dDurationMs": 3089,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "discussion on the topic in the comments."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 987769,
    "dDurationMs": 5351,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Appletank8 suggests exploring whether there’s\na form of photosynthesis or chemosynthesis"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 993120,
    "dDurationMs": 7110,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that can break up SiO2, noting that if even\nthe bottom of the food chain doesn’t work,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1000230,
    "dDurationMs": 5669,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "then nothing else will either. This is an excellent insight, and is one of the key reasons why"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1005899,
    "dDurationMs": 6870,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "silicon-based life may well be impossible.\nIn fact there is no known enzyme capable of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1012769,
    "dDurationMs": 6951,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "breaking the silicon dioxide molecule. That\nmeans the end of the silicon-based chemical"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1019720,
    "dDurationMs": 7020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "pathway may really be a dead end. Silica may\nsimply be too stable, and so silicon is too"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1026740,
    "dDurationMs": 8310,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "easily locked out of circulation for any evolutionarily-plausible\nbiochemistry. Compare that to carbon dioxide,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1035050,
    "dDurationMs": 5310,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which is relatively easily broken to get the\ncarbon back into circulation. Perhaps there"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1040360,
    "dDurationMs": 3880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are novel ways to release the silicon from\nsilicon dioxide that we haven’t thought"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1044240,
    "dDurationMs": 6830,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of yet - but there’s no question that the\ncarbon base is a much easier path to life,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1051070,
    "dDurationMs": 3560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and so it will out-compete silicon in most\ncircumstances."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1054630,
    "dDurationMs": 6780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "FatherDragonKal asks why not Boron or Nitrogen\nbased life as well? The main reason is simply"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1061410,
    "dDurationMs": 5020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that boron and nitrogen don’t form long\nmolecular chains with themselves, so they"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1066430,
    "dDurationMs": 5770,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "can’t form scaffolding structures for a\nwide variety of molecules. Sulfur can form"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1072200,
    "dDurationMs": 5770,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "long 1-D chains with itself, but can’t append\nmultiple molecules due to it only having two"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1077970,
    "dDurationMs": 6650,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "covalent bonds. Carbon and silicon can form\n1-D and 2-D structures and append multiple"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1084620,
    "dDurationMs": 5480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "other atoms, but of these only carbon has\nthe various properties that make it ideal"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1090100,
    "dDurationMs": 3240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for life - all the stuff we talked about in\nthat episode."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1093340,
    "dDurationMs": 5980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Ƿynnťari asks whether we’ve considered\nlife that isn’t similar to Earth life. The"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1099320,
    "dDurationMs": 4340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "answer is yes, but there’s a reason we tend\nto focus on at least chemical life. Science"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1103660,
    "dDurationMs": 5730,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fiction is full of ideas, from sentient oceans in Solaris to nuclear life"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1109390,
    "dDurationMs": 5210,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on neutron stars in Robert Forward’s Dragon’s Egg. But scientists have also tried to come"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1114600,
    "dDurationMs": 6860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "up with a non-chemical basis for life. Like\nself-sustaining data structures in crystals,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1121460,
    "dDurationMs": 6020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or magnetic monopoles on cosmic strings inside\nstars - we covered that once, or dust particles"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1127480,
    "dDurationMs": 5809,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "suspended in plasma. But everything I’ve\never heard of king of feels like a stretch"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1133289,
    "dDurationMs": 5881,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- all of it seems way harder than good ol’ chemical, and preferably carbon based life."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1139170,
    "dDurationMs": 6700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You know what, let me tell you what I really think about this - The apparent fine tuning of this universe"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1145870,
    "dDurationMs": 6110,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "strongly suggests to me that there’s a multiverse\nof some type of other, and the vast majority"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1151980,
    "dDurationMs": 4939,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of universes are uninhabitable. We naturally\nobserve a universe that happens to have the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1156919,
    "dDurationMs": 7321,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "qualities to make at least one form of life\npossible. But the chance for a given universe"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1164240,
    "dDurationMs": 7140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to be tuned so finely that multiple forms\nof life are possible is very close to zero."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1171380,
    "dDurationMs": 6010,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So yeah, other forms of life exist out there\n- but “out there” probably means several"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1177390,
    "dDurationMs": 1560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "universes away."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1178950,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Many, many people noticed that the molecule\nwe showed for nitroglycerine was actually"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1184070,
    "dDurationMs": 6710,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "TNT. And, as Benedictul points out, these\nare definitely not the same thing despite"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1190780,
    "dDurationMs": 7390,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the misinformation spread by AC/DC - accadacca\nto their fiends and compatriots. It’s incredibly"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1198170,
    "dDurationMs": 4460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "impressive that there are so many of you that can immediately distinguish these molecules"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1202630,
    "dDurationMs": 4890,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "at a glance. As you can tell, I’m not in\nthat elite group. I do apologize and will"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1207520,
    "dDurationMs": 3550,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "try to memorize more chemical structures for the future."
    } ]
  } ]
}
